# Security & compliance posture

Relevant role specs: `.claude/agents/cybersecurity-architect.md`, `.claude/agents/security-engineer.md`,
`.claude/agents/compliance-specialist.md`.

## Standing rulings already made — apply these, don't re-derive

- **Every path that mints a backend session for an account with a verified MFA factor must check
  for that factor before minting — no exceptions per surface.** This was a real, shipped bug:
  `POST /auth/login` enforced it, `POST /auth/supabase/exchange` (the customer web login path)
  didn't, and it took a Stage 8 pass to catch. If you add a new session-minting path (a new OAuth
  provider, a new exchange route, anything that calls `mintNewSession`), it must call the same
  verified-factor check `/auth/login` uses before minting, not after.
- **Cross-account credential-collision handling (one physical credential — a push token, a
  device id, anything similarly unverifiable — claimed by a second account) uses a
  deferred-takeover pattern, not an inline demotion.** Established via `backend/src/lib/push-token-takeover.ts`:
  quarantine the *new* claim (disabled, timestamped) rather than immediately disabling the prior
  owner's row; only complete the takeover after a cooldown with no resolution; write a real,
  queryable audit record structurally incapable of holding the sensitive credential itself; email
  the losing account once. An inline "last write wins" demotion on an unverified credential is
  the anti-pattern this replaced — don't reintroduce it on a new surface.
- **A device-binding check that can be silently skipped (`if (session?.deviceId && …)`) is a
  fail-open bug, not a defensible default.** Fixed instance: push-token registration now rejects
  a session with no bound device rather than exempting it. Any new check keyed on an optional
  session field should reject on absence, not skip.
- **No server-side biometric MFA factor** may ever be enrolled against the Supabase identity
  project — POPIA s57 prior-authorization risk. On-device biometrics (Face ID/Touch ID/
  passkeys where the template never leaves the device) are fine; the platform must never
  receive or store a biometric template itself.
- **No Supabase AI-assistant/LLM feature** may be enabled against the identity project — an
  OpenAI sub-processor entry was found on Supabase's own sub-processor list with no way to
  confirm it doesn't touch identity data. Same standing prohibition extends to any SMTP
  vendor's AI features (Brevo's DPA names AI sub-processors too).
- **No Supabase log drain to a third-party destination** without a fresh compliance review —
  each one is a new, undocumented transborder PII flow.
- **Any new third-party operator touching PII** (a new SMTP vendor, analytics tool, error-
  tracking service, etc.) needs the same treatment `compliance-review-supabase.md` gave
  Supabase: an operator/s72 lawfulness analysis, a written DPA, a retention check, and an
  entry in the RoPA (`compliance-review-supabase.md` C-8) — not a separate untracked record.
  See `compliance-review-smtp-vendor.md` for the second worked example of this method.
- **Token TTLs for anything that leaves the platform's own store** (email links, especially)
  must stay short enough that a copy retained in a third-party vendor's logs is inert — see
  C-5.3's reasoning (60min reset / 24h verification / 72h invitation) if adding a new token
  type that transits a vendor.

## Open items as of this handoff (check `HANDOFF.md` for current status before assuming closed)

- **Contract-driven POPIA obligations (new, 2026-08-28)** —
  `docs/organization/10-data-protection-contract-obligations.md` records obligations from
  contract TDIT-2026-09 §19 layered on top of the existing POPIA analysis: NextWave is an
  **Operator**, TD IT Solution is the **Responsible Party**; a 48-hour breach-notice clock to
  the Client; **no cross-border transfer without the Client's prior written consent** (flagged
  as a confirmed gap — Render Frankfurt hosting and the Supabase EU region currently have no
  such consent on file); and a test-data anonymise-or-delete requirement. Read this before
  making any claim about hosting region, breach process, or test-data handling being compliant.
- Supabase DPA execution — was still unexecuted as of last check; a hard blocker on real
  production use, not just a nice-to-have.
- ADR-0006 (privileged-access audit correlation) — **ratified 2026-08-11 (§16), with §17
  appended as a post-ratification correction.** Its migrations (032, 033) are applied to the
  live Supabase project.
- ADR-0009 (self-asserted location ingestion trust boundary, Feature 008) — **Ratified**
  conditionally by `cto` 2026-08-24 (§17); `security-engineer` (§15) and `compliance-specialist`
  (§16) concurrences remain reserved/unfiled. Its SDL-1…SDL-12 requirements are binding under
  `cybersecurity-architect`'s standing authority regardless. **Its own §14 prohibition on
  shipping any ingestion code was breached** — the endpoint shipped to production and a
  distributed preview APK before Stage 8 ran. See **INC-001**
  (`docs/organization/incidents/INC-001-location-ingestion.md`): contained via a server-side +
  client-side kill switch, **not closed**. As of 2026-08-28, `cybersecurity-architect` has
  **withheld** Release Gate A criterion 6 sign-off (no surface that bypassed Stage 8 is
  reachable in a client build) — see INC-001 §9.4 and ADR-0009 §18.7 — so build #2 does not
  ship until that's resolved. Does **not** reopen or amend ADR-0006 — read both, plus INC-001,
  before doing anything with location data.
- AUD-3 (bulk admin list calls not recording a per-subject audit entry) — shape ruled (R-1);
  **closed on both trails** — Trail A (`GET /v1/admin/accounts` calls `recordBulkDisclosure()`)
  and Trail B (`GET /v1/admin/policies*`/`GET /v1/admin/assets*` write to `admin_access_log` via
  `admin-access-log.ts`; bootstrap in `feature004-collections.ts`). Live Atlas verification
  (SR-004-admin-2) still pending — check `HANDOFF.md` before assuming that's closed too. Any new
  admin list endpoint must write one row per disclosed subject **plus** one call-scoped row;
  C-14 binds to every such endpoint.
- **FU-A13** — `account_audit_log_account_id_created_at` and the partial
  `account_audit_log_created_at` were applied via migration 034 (2026-08-11), catalog-verified.
  Purge scheduling is still not wired — `app.purge_expired_audit_log()` exists but nothing calls
  it. Retention is not enforced today, whatever a compliance review says.
- **FU-A14** — AUD-9 requires a purpose/case reference on location and partner-operator access
  that resolves to a real case. **The entity half is now discharged** — `recovery_cases` exists
  (`recovery.ts`, `security-cases.ts`). What's still open is the **wiring half** (no read path
  resolves a purpose against a case yet, no location endpoint exists to attach one to — ADR-0009
  §2 tracks this as SD-FU-07). A free-text purpose string remains explicitly ruled out.
- **Features 006/007 Stage 8** — both now have a real, multi-round `security-review.md` with
  chair sign-off and partial joint concurrence (not the "no Stage 8 exists" state from earlier).
  `security-engineer` has concurred on the technical findings; `compliance-specialist` has
  concurred *in part* and withholds concurrence on real customer PI pending the Supabase DPA, a
  Resend-specific operator review (the vendor changed from Brevo and was never re-reviewed), and
  a platform-level RoPA — none of which exist in this repo. Don't treat either feature as clear
  for real customer accounts on the strength of "Stage 8 passed" alone; check the conditions.
- **SR-007-11 — stale, corrected.** This used to say no admin account-suspend/deactivate
  endpoint existed. That was itself a false negative-verification (see INC-001 §5, case 1):
  `PATCH /v1/admin/accounts/:id/state` (`backend/src/routes/admin-accounts.ts`) is built and
  test-covered, with RBAC, audit logging, session revocation, and push-token disablement. Don't
  repeat the old claim, and don't assume this means it has a filed Stage 8 record just because
  it's built — verify against `docs/features/**/security-review*.md` before citing it as
  cleared.

## Process

Stage 8 (Security Review) and Stage 10 (QA Testing) in the 15-stage lifecycle
(`docs/organization/02-feature-lifecycle.md`) are hard gates. A feature's `security-review.md`
verdict format is: sign-off granted / granted with required changes / withheld — check for an
existing one before assuming a feature is clear to build against in production.
